h lambda is the lambda at which it forms. So stability = sum_{p in cluster} (lambda_death(p) - lambda_birth). Then to select clusters, we start from leaves and propagate up, selecting a cluster if its stability is greater than the sum of stabilities of its children.\n\nGiven the input: clusters array contains all clusters, each with size, birth_lambda, and point_exit_lambdas (list of exit lambdas for points in that cluster? But why length mismatch?). The parent mapping gives the parent for each cluster (c0 has no parent, c1 and c2 parent c0). So it's a tree.\n\nPerhaps the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points exit the cluster? But the number of exit lambdas may not equal the size because some points persist beyond the cluster's death? In HDBSCAN, the cluster dies when its last point exits. So the cluster's death lambda is the maximum of its points' death lambdas. But the point_exit_lambdas could be the list of all death lambdas for points in the cluster. For c0, size 7 but only 3 exit lambdas; maybe the other 4 points are still alive at the end? Or perhaps the point_exit_lambdas are for the points that exit before the cluster's death? In the condensed tree, points can exit at various lambdas; some may exit at the same lambda as cluster death. The list might contain each point's exit lambda, but if multiple points exit at exactly the same lambda, they could be repeated? In the example, c0 has 3 exit lambdas, c1 has 3, c2 has 1. The sizes: c0=7, c1=4, c2=2. That doesn't add up. Possibly the sum of sizes is 13, but total points? Could be that points are shared? Actually, in a hierarchical clustering, points belong to multiple clusters along the path. But then the point_exit_lambdas might represent different things. Alternatively, maybe the clusters are not disjoint in terms of points; each cluster represents a set of points that are together at a certain scale. The parent relationship indicates that children are subsets? Actually c0 contains c1 and c2 as 